# America/Nuuk as tzdata modelled Greenland before the 2023a rule change:
# -03:00 standard, -02:00 in summer, transitioning on the EU schedule at
# 01:00 UTC. Under these rules 2026-03-28 23:30 local is an ordinary wall
# clock the zone observes at -03:00 — there is no gap there at all.
#
# bin/build-stale-zoneinfo compiles this over a copy of the host's zoneinfo
# directory to produce the one configuration a real host can reach but CI
# otherwise cannot construct: system-shaped data that is *also* stale on a
# rule the suite asserts. The copy inherits the host's modelling and links
# state, which varies by image, and the capability-gated tests in
# test/capabilities/ absorb that difference. Either way this is the
# combination where a stale database answers confidently and wrongly rather
# than raising, which is the harder of the two failure modes to attribute.
#
# This deliberately shadows a real identifier, unlike test/fixtures/tz/*.zi,
# whose `Fixture/`-prefixed zones exist precisely so they cannot be mistaken
# for real ones. Shadowing is the point here: the environment has to be a
# plausible stale host, not a synthetic zone nothing asserts against.
#
# Note the scope within Nuuk: the Zone line below replaces the zone's *entire*
# history with these rules, not just the post-2023a ones. Harmless for what
# stale_vintage_test.rb asserts, which only looks at 2026. Across the tree the
# replacement is narrow, though — every other zone in the copied directory
# (America/New_York's 2007 rules, Australia/Lord_Howe's 30-minute gap,
# Europe/Dublin's modelling) stays exactly as the host has it.
Rule	StaleGl	1981	max	-	Mar	lastSun	1:00u	1:00	-02
Rule	StaleGl	1996	max	-	Oct	lastSun	1:00u	0	-03
Zone	America/Nuuk	-3:00	StaleGl	%s
